    body: '다음 글에서 필자가 주장하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In many conversations, saying “I don’t know” feels like a small defeat. Students fear it will make them look unprepared, and professionals worry that it will damage their reputation. So people guess, offer vague answers, or repeat something they half-remember with great confidence. The trouble is that a confident wrong answer can do far more harm than an honest admission of ignorance. A doctor who guesses instead of ordering another test, or a manager who invents a figure instead of checking it, may lead others into serious mistakes. Moreover, pretending to know closes the door to learning, since no one looks for an answer they believe they already have. Saying “I don’t know, but I will find out” is not a sign of weakness. It shows that you care more about getting things right than about appearing clever. We should feel free to admit what we do not know and treat it as the first step toward finding out.</div>',
