    body: '밑줄 친 <u>drawing a map as large as the land</u>가 다음 글에서 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Imagine a mapmaker so devoted to accuracy that she refuses to leave anything out. Every tree, every stone, and every puddle must appear on her map, exactly where it is. To include all of this, she has to make the map bigger and bigger, until at last it is as large as the land itself. Such a map would be perfectly accurate and completely useless. No one could fold it, carry it, or find a route on it. A map is valuable precisely because it ignores most of the world and shows only what the traveler needs. The same is true of a summary, a model, or a scientific theory. An economist describing a market or a teacher explaining the water cycle must decide what to leave out. Those who insist on including every detail end up <u>drawing a map as large as the land</u>, and their audience learns nothing from it.</div>',
