    body: '다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We tend to believe that we taste food with our tongues alone, but our eyes play a surprisingly large role. In a number of experiments, researchers have given people drinks whose colors did not match their flavors, such as a cherry-flavored drink colored orange. Many participants identified the flavor by its color rather than by its actual taste, reporting that the orange drink tasted of orange. Color can also affect how strong a flavor seems: a more deeply colored drink is often judged to be sweeter or more intense than an identical drink with a paler color. Food companies are well aware of this, which is why they carefully choose the colors of their products, sometimes adding coloring to make juices or candies look the way customers expect. Before the first bite, our eyes have already told our brains what to expect, and the brain often tastes what it expects.</div>',
