    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We usually assume that when we explain our own choices, we are reporting on processes we can directly observe. Experiments on choice suggest a more uncomfortable picture. In one well-known study, shoppers were asked to pick the best of four pairs of stockings that were, unknown to them, identical. The pair on the far right was chosen far more often than the others, apparently because of its position, yet none of the shoppers mentioned position when asked why. Instead, they pointed to the knit, the softness, or the sheerness of their chosen pair, and they rejected the suggestion that position had played any role. In related studies by the same researchers, moreover, participants’ explanations closely resembled the reasons that people who had never taken part guessed a participant would give. This suggests that the shoppers were not looking inward at all; they were drawing on the same shared theories about why people prefer things that anyone else might use. In explaining ourselves, then, we often ________.</div>',
