    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Before the printing press, English spelling was remarkably free. A single scribe might write the same word in two or three different ways on one page, and no one considered this an error.</div><div class="passage">(A) To do so, some of them seem to have added or dropped letters as space required, putting a final <i>e</i> on a word in one line and leaving it off in the next. Over time, however, the sheer number of identical copies produced by the presses made certain spellings far more visible than others.<br>(B) Readers who saw the same form again and again came to regard it as the correct one, and variation that had once been ordinary began to look like ignorance. Standard spelling, in other words, was less invented than gradually accumulated through repetition.<br>(C) Printing gradually changed this attitude, though not for the reasons one might expect. Early printers were not trying to reform the language; many of them were simply trying to fit text neatly onto each line.</div>',
