    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We tend to treat forgetting as a failure of memory, a leak in a container that ought to hold everything we put into it.</div><div class="passage">(A) A mind that stored every detail of every event would face a similar difficulty. Asked whether an unfamiliar animal is dangerous, it would have to search through thousands of particular memories, each slightly different, instead of consulting a general idea of what dangerous animals are like.<br>(B) By letting go of the details that differ from one experience to the next, forgetting leaves behind what those experiences had in common. In this sense, it is less a leak than a filter, one that turns scattered episodes into usable knowledge.<br>(C) Yet a memory that kept everything would not necessarily serve us better. Consider a library that never discards anything, not even duplicate copies or outdated drafts. As it grows, finding the one book you need becomes harder, not easier.</div>',
