    body: '다음 글에서 필자가 주장하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many parents try to protect their children from every financial mistake. They buy what the child needs, refuse what seems unwise, and step in before an allowance can be wasted. The intention is kind, but it only postpones a lesson that will be learned sooner or later, and the later it comes, the more it costs. A ten-year-old who spends a whole month’s allowance on a toy that breaks within a week loses a few dollars and gains a vivid memory of regret. A twenty-five-year-old who learns the same lesson with a credit card may lose far more. The cheapest time to discover that money runs out is when the amounts are small and a parent is nearby to help make sense of the experience. So when your child wants to spend her savings on something you are sure she will regret, tell her what you think once, and then let her decide. Afterward, do not rescue her by replacing the money; talk with her about what happened instead. The regret is the lesson, and paying for her mistake takes the lesson away.</div>',
