    slot: '글의 요지 · 고난도',
    body: '다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When musicians practice a difficult passage, the temptation is to play it at full speed again and again, hoping that repetition will eventually smooth out the mistakes. But each time a passage is played with an error, the error is rehearsed along with the correct notes, and the hands learn both. Experienced teachers therefore ask for something that feels like going backward: play the passage so slowly that every note can be placed correctly, and raise the tempo only in small steps, never beyond the point at which accuracy begins to slip. This approach is frustrating at first, because for a long time it produces nothing that sounds like music. Yet a passage built correctly at a slow speed tends to hold together when it is finally played fast, while a passage hammered out at full speed often falls apart under the pressure of a performance. Speed, in other words, is not something to be practiced directly; it is what accuracy becomes once it has been repeated enough.</div>',
