    body: '다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Each year, a tree in a temperate climate adds a new layer of wood beneath its bark. Wood formed early in the growing season is usually pale, while wood formed later is denser and darker, so each year’s growth appears as a distinct ring. The width of these rings is not constant. In a year with plenty of moisture and a long growing season, a tree may lay down a wide ring; in a year of severe drought, the ring may be barely visible. Because trees of the same species in the same region respond to the same weather, their rings share a pattern of wide and narrow years, a pattern distinctive enough that a beam from an old building can be dated by matching its rings to those of living trees and older timber. By linking such overlapping sequences, researchers have built records that reach back thousands of years, revealing droughts and cold periods that no one ever wrote down. A tree, in this sense, keeps a diary it never meant to keep.</div>',
