    body: '다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When choosing a present, most givers try to surprise. They pass over the item the recipient has openly asked for and search for something more original, believing that a gift chosen without help shows more thought and care. Recipients, however, tend to see the matter differently. Studies of gift giving have found that people are generally more pleased to receive something they explicitly requested than something the giver picked on their own, and that they do not regard the requested gift as less thoughtful. The mismatch arises partly because the two sides focus on different moments. The giver imagines the instant of unwrapping, when a surprise has its greatest effect. The recipient lives with the gift for months afterward, when what matters is whether it is actually useful. A wish list, then, is not a limit on the giver’s imagination but a piece of information the recipient has kindly provided. Ignoring it in the name of thoughtfulness often puts the giver’s wish to impress ahead of the recipient’s wish to be pleased.</div>',
