    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Architects are usually praised for buildings that look complete on the day they open, with every detail resolved and every space assigned its purpose. Yet a building’s opening day is the beginning of its life, not the end. Over the following decades, its users will want things its designers never imagined: a classroom becomes an office, a warehouse becomes a set of apartments, a single large hall is divided into small rooms. Buildings fitted too perfectly to their original purpose resist such changes. Their walls carry loads that make them impossible to move, their wiring is buried where no one can reach it, and every alteration damages the whole. Plainer buildings, with open floors and pipes and cables that can be reached and replaced, often fare better; they are adapted rather than torn down, and they may outlast their more admired neighbors by a century. A good building, from this point of view, is one that ________.</div>',
