    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When we want to understand what another person thinks or feels, the usual advice is to put ourselves in their shoes. Imagining the world from another’s position may make us more sympathetic, but it is surprisingly poor at making us accurate. The trouble is that the shoes we imagine ourselves into are still filled with our own feet. Asked to predict how a colleague will react to a decision, we picture ourselves in her situation and report how we would react, with our own tastes, fears, and history quietly substituted for hers. In studies where people tried to guess a partner’s opinions and preferences, those told to imagine the partner’s point of view were generally no more accurate than those given no instructions, and some became more confident without becoming more correct. What did improve accuracy was far simpler: asking the partner and listening to the answer. Understanding another mind, it seems, depends less on ________ than on a willingness to be told.</div>',
