    body: '[1~2] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.<div class="passage">In 1906, the scientist Francis Galton visited a country fair where people paid a small fee to guess the weight of an ox. Some of them were butchers and farmers, but many had no special knowledge of cattle, and plenty of the guesses were far off. Yet when Galton later examined the entries, he found that the middle value of all the guesses came remarkably <u>(a) close</u> to the true weight, closer than the great majority of the individual guesses. The crowd, taken together, seemed to know more than almost any of its members.<br>The explanation is not that crowds are mysteriously wise. Every guess contains some truth and some error. When the errors point in different directions, some too high and some too low, they tend to <u>(b) cancel</u> out as the guesses are combined, while the truth that each guess contains remains. This is why the method works only under a particular condition: the errors must be <u>(c) independent</u> of one another. Galton’s fairgoers each wrote a guess on a ticket, drawing on their own impression of the animal.<br>Suppose instead that each guesser had heard the earlier guesses announced aloud. Early guesses would then pull later ones toward them, and the errors would begin to lean the same way. Adding more people would no longer <u>(d) correct</u> the mistake; it would only repeat it with greater confidence. A crowd whose members influence one another can thus be <u>(e) more</u> reliable than a single careful individual, since it repeats one error many times while appearing to combine many judgments.<br>The lesson matters well beyond country fairs. Committees, markets, and online ratings all gather the views of many people, and all of them are tempted to share information early and openly. Sometimes this helps. But a group that wants to benefit from the knowledge of its members should first collect their judgments separately, before discussion begins, so that what each person knows is counted rather than drowned out.</div><p>밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?</p>',
