    slot: '빈칸 추론 · 고난도',
    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Pleasant experiences tend to fade as they continue. The first bite of a dessert is delicious; the tenth is merely sweet. Psychologists call this adaptation, and it helps explain a common mistake. People usually expect breaks to spoil their enjoyment: they assume that a massage interrupted halfway, or a television program broken up by commercials, must be less pleasant than one that flows without a pause. Yet studies have found something surprising. People who watched programs with commercial breaks often enjoyed the programs more than people who watched them straight through, even though they disliked the commercials themselves. The break did not add any pleasure of its own; what it did was to interrupt the process by which pleasure wears off, so that the experience, when it resumed, felt partly new again. We fail to predict this because we imagine an experience as a fixed amount of enjoyment from which a break can only subtract. In fact, enjoyment depends heavily on how fresh an experience feels, and so the very interruptions we try to avoid may be ________.</div>',
