    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">It is common to speak of genes as a blueprint, as if they specified a body in the way an architect’s drawing specifies a building. Consider, however, the water flea <i>Daphnia</i>, a tiny animal that lives in ponds. When young water fleas grow up in water that carries chemical traces of their predators, many of them develop helmet-like heads or spines that make them harder to catch and swallow. Raised in water without those traces, genetically identical individuals grow up without such defenses, which are thought to be costly to build. The difference cannot be put down to different genes, since the animals share the same ones; nor can it be said that the environment simply pressed a passive body into shape, since the response is precise and useful. What the genes provide is not a single form but something closer to a rule: if predators are present, build armor. A gene, on this view, is less like a drawing of a finished building than like ________.</div>',
