    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When we catch a cold or the flu, we usually treat a fever as part of the illness, something to be brought down as quickly as possible.</div><div class="passage">(A) Some of the microbes that infect us multiply best at normal body temperature, and a rise of a degree or two can slow them down. Higher temperatures also appear to speed up some of the body’s own defenses.<br>(B) From this point of view, a fever is not something the illness does to us but something we do to the illness. This does not mean that fevers should never be treated, since a very high fever can itself be dangerous; but it does mean that a moderate one may be doing useful work.<br>(C) Yet a fever is not simply a breakdown in the body’s temperature control. It is a deliberate change: the brain raises the temperature at which the body is set, much as a person might turn up a thermostat. Why would the body do this?</div>',
